\begin{proof}
Let $\mathfrak{F}$ be the family of all topologies on $X$ containing $\mathcal{A}$, and put $\mathcal{O}=\bigcap_{\mathcal{S}\in\mathfrak{F}}\mathcal{S}$. The family $\mathfrak{F}$ is nonempty because the discrete topology belongs to it, and $\mathcal{O}$ is a topology because arbitrary intersections of topologies are topologies.

By definition, $\mathcal{O}$ is contained in every member of $\mathfrak{F}$. Since the topology $\mathcal{T}$ generated by $\mathcal{A}$ contains $\mathcal{A}$, $\mathcal{T}\in\mathfrak{F}$, and hence $\mathcal{O}\subseteq\mathcal{T}$.

On the other hand, $\mathcal{O}$ is itself a topology containing $\mathcal{A}$. Since $\mathcal{T}$ is the topology generated by the basis $\mathcal{A}$, every member of $\mathcal{T}$ is a union of members of $\mathcal{A}$. Those basis elements lie in $\mathcal{O}$, and $\mathcal{O}$ is closed under arbitrary unions. Thus $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{O}$. Therefore $\mathcal{T}=\mathcal{O}$.
\end{proof}
